> Have you beaten Nethack ??  Slash'Em is much harder, start with the
> easier(ha!) Nethack

I strongly endorse this advice.  Consider SlashEM to be grad school for
Nethack.  I played something like 10 years of Nethack before I got an
ascension.  After ascending 10 different classes on Nethack, it started
becoming a little repetitive (the "challenge yourself by strictly
avoiding some useful behavior" never appealed to me).

I decided to move to SlashEM.  I've been playing for several months,
and have taken exactly 1 character as far as Gehennom.  I'd guess only
about 1 in 8 of my characters get significantly past Minetown and
Sokoban.  Maybe fewer.

Get to where you can ascend a character in Nethack with some
regularity.  I would call this "learning the fundamentals".  Then you
might be ready to start playing SlashEM.  That's been my experience
anyway.

That said here's some SlashEM advice.  Mostly it's lessons I've
recently learned or am experimenting with at the moment.

The main difficulty (over Nethack) I notice is how tough some (but not
most) of the monsters are compared to you.  You're feeling pretty
comfortable at your dungeon level, and then discover that some monster
hits way harder and/or is way harder to hit than you expected.  Often
these monsters end up moving faster than you too.  It also *seems* more
likely that you run into monsters polymorphed into ridiculously
difficult monsters for the dungeion level.  I'd say I die by running
out of hitpoints on SlashEM *far* more often than I did on Nethack.

1) Have a retreat to safety plan on the ready.  Have a few.  Any
possible way you can cloister yourself from baddies, leave the current
level or teleport out of danger, have it in the back of your mind.
Stay close to the stairs when starting to explore a new level.
Regularly use telepathy and a blindfold to scope out the level and what
(minded) monsters are on it.  If you see (by any means) a monster(s)
you even remotely think might be tougher and faster than you, move that
retreat to safety plan to the front of your mind.

2) Missile weapons or attack are essential.  Collect and highgrade
missile weapons appropriate to your class.  If your missile weapons are
non-destructible (i.e. daggers), pelt *any* hostile monster as it
approaches.   If it's destructible, use it often, until your supply
starts getting low.  Keeping your HP as high as possible in this manner
can only help.

3) Keeping your hitpoints near their max is vital.  I used to (loosely)
use a 25% runaway strategy in Nethack, but I use more like a 66%
strategy in SlashEM.  Never adventure forward if you're not at near max
HP, find a (relatively) safe place and heal up (wait around with the .
key).  Horde food and eat safe corpses to make sure you don't get undue
pressure to explore ahead for hunger's sake if you're not appropriately
healed.

4) You don't have to kill monsters.  Teleport them away.  Get them
stuck/hurt in a trap.  Close (and even lock) doors.  Retreat to a
"cleared" level, cloister yourself away and Heal up, explore a
different path, or even just level up by killing randomly generated
creatures on a level you've already cleared, until you're more able to
confront the challenge you ran away from.  Best of all, tame or charm
them (research various ways).  If you're desperate polymorph them.
Wield or throw a helpful potion.  Use conflict (or confusion) to get
them to kill each other.  Any other damn thing you can think of.  Avoid
swarms by fighting from tight places with limited access (i.e.
corridors).  At the first *glimmer* of trouble, do a scan of your
inventory, spells, techniques and intrinsics and think "in what,
obscure and tangential as necessary, way could any of this help me out
before proceeding?  Think of the dungeon floor layout in the same way.
Spend as much time as seems reasonable.  If the danger escalates or
changes, spend some more.  It's not a real-time game.

5) Watch / talk to / post with people that are better than you.  I
believe you can watch other players games on the SlashEM servers.  I
think there is an IRC channel as well, where I imagine you could get
advice *while* you were playing. There are *years* of posts in this
newsgroup, and getting a feel for people's approaches, attitudes and
mistakes is invaluable.  If you find a specific trouble is often the
death of you, research it online and/or read up on the spoilers.  I had
read many spoilers on Nethack, but after switching to SlashEM, decided
that I was going to remain unspoiled on the differences, to keep
SlashEM fresh.  Even that little bit of idealism has started to slide
away.

6) Explore different strategies, as widely as possible.  Is this
mindset of mine or approach or strategy *really* necessary?  Admitting
that "What seems reasonable to me, and/or served me well at an earlier
point in my learning Nethack/SlashEM" may not be useful, or may even be
harmful where you are now, both in terms of knowledge as well as new
(i.e. deeper) parts of the dungeon.  Devote games (or even all games
for a period of time) to focussing on a strategy or approach.  Have a
game where you let your pets (as many as possible?) do as much damage
as possible, and you as little as possible.  See what you learn.  Try
to thoroughly rob every shop you get to.  See what you learn.  Devote
inordinate amouts of time to pleasing your god.  See what you learn.
Try to make a spellcaster a fighting machine, or a fighter a
spellcaster.  See what you learn.  See how long you can stay alive
without leaving dungeon level 1 (or going no deeper than dungeon level
X).  See what you learn.  Explore different classes special
weapons/techniques/intrinsics etc., and focus on one ascpect of them.
Playing a drow, and unwielding your weapon to put monsters to sleep,
and then rewielding it to kick their ass rules.  I discovered it by
accident.  Try playing classes that start with plenty of food, or
plenty of money.  See if removing these challenges helps.  Try to focus
on polymorphing, yourself, your enemies, items if the opportunity
presents itself.  See what you learn.

7) Get and use Hearse.  It's always encouraging to see your character
continue past where some other poor slob bit it.  And you may get
equipment you're not used to seeing as well.  Is it just me, or does
SlashEM create fewer bones files than Nethack?

8) My personal start out strategy is to proceed to the Mall.  Then,
especially if I haven't found an altar, go to minetown.  Finding
something especially useful in the early multi-shop levels can shape
your early strategy.  Then go to Sokoban for the gift.  After this I'll
take turns going deeper in minetown and deeper in the main dungeon,
working on the other branch if I run into trouble on the first.  I
don't think the luckstone at the bottom of the gnomish mines is worth
an early push, and you find one lying around often enough anyway.

I hope some of this at least gives you a little hope if it doesn't help
outright.  

